d up the points  road water supply  to the west eaton water supply and one  of the drivers for that was we were  concerned about the nitrate levels on  the  points road  scheme  it was uh it was getting too close for  our like liking um to the trigger levels  and the drinking water standards  um so we're uh  so in terms of nitrates we're now  getting most of our water um  i'd probably go as far as about 99  percent of it from very deep aquifers  that um to date have not had any issues  with nitrates  when you look out over a one sort of a  thing like a 100-year window  some of the work that has been done by  environment canterbury does show that  there is a potential  risk to  those very deep aquifers now that's  being managed  by the rural sector and um through the  regional plan that environment canada  and canterbury do  so and there's also a lot more work and  modeling going into trying to get a much  better understanding of that so it's  very unlikely that it's going to become  an issue for us a large issue for us  our water treatment that we do um  on a lot of our scans we actually don't  need to do any water treatment  but if we were to require to do that it  would be quite some time out well beyond  2050 and it's actually  with all of the um land use activity and  and the changes in the rural sector  we're hoping that that actually never  eventuates that  nitrates are managed at the source  rather than becoming an issue for future  generations um look if it does there are  ways of treating water to manage it and  they can't they actually it can be quite  expensive  um so the short answer is we've we've  got that in hand um it's something we're  monitoring um and we don't see that  there's going to be affordability issue  for our district with regard to nitrates  um climate change  we've been thinking about climate change  for a long time so um  and we're we're currently spending about  20 million dollars in kaiapoi um  upgrading the stormwater infrastructure  so we're putting in some quite large um  trunk stormwater upgrades so part of the  driver for that is all is not only  managing  the issues we've got at the moment but  it's actually to help  provide some protection for kaya puya  against climate change  we've modeled  all of our flood mapping has an  allowance for increase in rainfall we've  allowed for increased storm surge at the  coast at a higher sea level  so we're really thinking about it so  everything that's been built into our  long-term plan and our infrastructure  strategy does make allowance for climate  change um  and  but it is one of these things that's  about that is evolving over time and no  matter what happens whether it's the  council or the um you know a large  entity it's going to come more and more  to the fore in our district particularly  for the low-lying and coastal  communities um  and that that is something that the  council will definitely be engaging with  the community um out into the future but  from a infrastructure point of view yes  we're we're planning for it we're  predicting and  uh and starting to budgets for it as  well  you can't just say that from a political  level  climate change is a is a really  important issue for the council we are  in the process and we have a climate  change strategy and we're working our  way through that  can we do more of course there's lots  more we can do but we're trying to  adequately plan as jared's mentioning  we're also working together with our  partner councils including environment  canterbury i personally chair  uh the canterbury mural forum climate  change group and it's looking at  information across our different  councils because if we're to to work on  this it's something that we've actually  got to work on across canterbury in fact  across new zealand uh because it's a  really important issue but at a local  level when i was elected  i wanted to signal the importance of  climate change and i pointed a council  to a councillor to the role of climate  change and sustainability um to signal  the importance of that and the council  that's leading that's doing a really  good job we've got lots more work we are  going to do in that space  but we are starting with earnest but as  jared said we're planning from an  infrastructural perspective but also  broader than that what our response will  be but working in at a regional level  and also at a national level  thanks jared and mia dan  uh richard wants to know do the council  have alternative options for reform of  three waters or prefer to continue with  status quo  jim would you like to answer  this i think that's probably a better  question for the mayor  look i  i'm happy to give that a go  um look at the present time we we we are  saying that that from our council's  perspective the status quo adequately  addresses where we're at because we've  made it clear that we've we've got  things uh  under control and managed but from a  regional perspective we're really happy  to work and then look at alternatives  and i've mentioned a couple that we've  been considering and what's what we're  saying to the government press the pause  get let the water regulator get in place  get it established then let's see where  we go and there's a number of options  including the nine scenarios that  the ceo mentioned earlier that the  government had had been considering i  mentioned the earlier around the  deprivation scale for instance that's  one way that the central pot could be  put aside and councils could apply to  that or a co-funding model like nzta  heaven place which we that's their  transport authority  and uh for instance we get 50 50 funding  uh on our roads and but we go into a bid  process for that at a national level i  think that and then number of mural  colleagues throughout the country and  councils think that's a good option to  explore where you could look at the  co-funding and then look at the likes of  the chathams for instance or other  communities which are struggling and  they may get  funding on a higher basis which is what  happens now with the ncta funding so a  co-funding model is something we  certainly think can be explored but  we've actually been working  uh on a regional basis anyway of looking  how we can share our services and work  together more effectively and that's  something that will continue regardless  of the reform discussion those those  discussions are happening now but from  our perspective we're not seeing  for our district that there that the  model is put forward or uh other than  the status quo of advantage to us at the  moment but we're open-minded to what  other future potentials may be developed  as long as the government allows us to  have that  to keep that decision  and make that choice ourselves  thank you dan  and we're hearing concerns about  chlorine being added to water supplies  and could jarrod you talk to this one  yeah look that's a really important  issue for our district  and that is something that the new um  water services bill will introduce as an  issue that'll um and it's already it's  already come up as an issue for us um  so  the vast majority of our urban schemes  do not get chlorinated  at the moment and when we have surveyed  our communities in the past we've had  very strong feedback  our community tell us they don't want  chlorine in the water on the whole  there are some people in our community  who do but  certainly based on the feedback we get  uh the majority of people prefer to have  their water without chlorine  so um  and we're very fortunate to be in a  position to be able to do that  because of the good quality of our  groundwater that we get  so the new  regulations will actually  make it compulsory for councils or  anyone supplying water  to chlorinate um they call it residual  disinfection but that just means  chlorine really um  and unless they get an exemption from  the regulator um now that is something  we're trying to work and we're working  closely with our neighbors and so when  in christchurch because they also have  the same facing the same issue they have  a strong desire to to provide a  non-chlorinated supply as their  community expect  and so we're being quite proactive in  that working together with them and we  want to really proactively engage with  the water regulator as soon as they are  up and running um and we've already  initiated  some level of conversation there  so that will be an issue for us um  i don't believe any um council in new  zealand has managed to get a water  safety plan approved without chlorine  just in the last year or two since this  has become such an issue um and that  puts us in that camp as well um so it  will come to the head for us um  very soon and that and and it's  important to note that's going to be an  issue regardless of  whether um  it's a  a bigger entity or your or your local  council who currently provides the water  um and that may be something that you  know in the feedback um we're expecting  people if you've got a view on  if these entities do stand up um  let us know what you you know whether  you want to have the ability for as a  community to still um look for that  level of service where you don't have  chlorine because there will be  additional  requirements that we're going to have to  do going into the future to be able to  continue to provide that non-chlorinated  water  and jared rightly observes that  chlorination is a big issue for our  community we  uh he and i both flew to wellington in  front of the select committee on the  water services bill and put this case  very strongly on behalf of our community  because we know how  important it is that's why we've made  the investment and deep source aquifers  over in making sure that we're providing  good drinking water and also that we  have been investigating alternatives  like uv disinfection  so you know we have applied for it for  an exemption and we will keep fighting  hard on or advocating hard on the  subject because we know how important it  is for our community but  in the hands of a of a um  a new water entity  not sure what say we'd have over there  that's one of the risks and ultimately  as jared says the government legislate  for it um through a and we and the water  regulator make that decision  what choice we will have apart from  strong advocacy which is what we will  always do on behalf of our ratepayers  thank you both and we just got a couple  more questions uh cass is interested to  know is council concerned with the  upcoming rma reforms affecting this  process and decision  uh jim perhaps over to you  that's an excellent question thank you  um the rma reforms  essentially  um are going to result in a regionalized  approach to a new spatial plan for  canterbury and then the natural built  environment act which will essentially  have the the rules for land use and  again  the uh the proposition is that that will  be prepared at a regional level  so you can sort of see a natural fit  between  a smaller number of water entities and a  regional approach to planning because  one of the crucial things here is the  alignment of our planning to the  infrastructure requirements and at the  moment  that's relatively easy for the council  to do  so our district planners and our  long-term planners and spatial planning  people work closely with jarrod and his  team around the infrastructure and  getting the timing right along with  developers so with the rma reforms  potentially being much set at a regional  level then that responsiveness to  the infrastructure needs and the  provision and the timing could be a  challenge  but also if these entities water  entities were created there would have  to be very strong linkages written into  them to formally participate in our  district planning processes even if it  is at a regional level  so  mia dan mentioned earlier about the  canterbury mayor's writing to the  minister of local government suggesting  there'd be a pause  essentially to allow all the reforms to  catch up because if you were doing it in  a logical sequence you would come up  with a future for local government  direction then you bring in the planning  direction and then you bring in the  infrastructure direction but we're  almost doing it the other way around  three waters reform first planning  second and then finally the future for  local government so  there's there needs to be some greater  work done to align  wherever the rma reforms are going with  the water reforms as a minimum and  ideally see it in the context of the  future for local government and i'm  pretty sure mayor dan will have a view  on this one as well  i think jim's very adequately  articulated that the the future for  local government discussion should have  happened first or should be happening  now  uh  actually rma reform as jim rightly  mentions has potentially got huge  consequences so it's the speed and pace  of this reform program that's happening  right at the moment that we are saying  there just needs to be a breather let's  catch up let's get the water regulator  in place future for local government  conversation then we can start to think  about where we're placed in that so we  can adequately consider the timing of  all this proposed change and what  response we can give at speed of it at  the moment that really concerns us  because there's only so much you can do  to keep up with all that  and water reforms on top of it it's just  not adequate time to to properly  consider and the government brought  forward the reform program  uh what was to be a conversation that we  were expecting to hear probably by  december we were advised uh back in july  that we were needing to have an advanced  conversation and give a position by the  end of september  um which  we just weren't prepared for so that's  why we're out with this conversation  we've got the material out the best  information we've got because we wanted  to have that conversation with our  community and set your view  so that we can adequately re represent  that opinion to government when required  to by the end of september  thanks dan  and caitlin wants to know what is the  opinion of other councils are there more  in support or against and perhaps dan  again back to you  i think there's a growing concern around  council there's certainly some councils  that do support the reform program  but the the growing view amongst  councils at the moment is that they're  disappointed with the government's  advertising campaign they're  disappointed with the speed and pace of  the reform  and what they're asking for in the  message of canterbury mural forum is  actually followed through a number of  councils who are saying let's press the  pause let's give them time to consider  it let the water regulator in place as  we've said and see what happens from  there  but no to wait there's not a united view  amongst councils and local government  new zealand are certainly  pushing for the reform conversation  but majority a number of councils that i  talk to are certainly concerned about it  and  want the whole process slowed down so we  can adequately consider it  but there will be councils that some  will benefit from it and some won't and  we're one of the councils that has  actually performed really well in that  space so we're not seeing  a benefit for us apart from  potentially subsidising other councils  which haven't performed but there are  other models as we've talked about  and those other councils i'm talking to  all the time would rather have that  conversation openly and consider what  those alternatives are before we're  stuck with a conversation about four  entities which is effectively where the  government's at the moment  thank you dan we're almost out of time  so if you have asked a question that we  didn't manage to get to this evening  don't worry we will reply online after  the session but before we go mayor dan  why is it important that people give us  their feedback  look it's really important to hear what  our residents have to say for us and to  be informed by that and that's why we've  gone ahead with this exercise of going  out with the survey that we posted to  you  or that you can fill the survey in  online the link  if you want to do that online is  waimakariri.gabt.nz forward slash let's  talk  by doing this the council will be well  informed and that we can give  waimakari's feedback to the government  it's really important that we hear your  views on this and that's why we put  together this conversation tonight to  inform you  if you've still got questions we welcome  uh you're you're contacting us directly  so we can answer those or fill in the  chat and we'll do our best to get that  information back to you but we really  welcome that feedback and your views the  survey closes  on september the 5th  so please do get your view in before  then  after that the council will  finalize what feedback that we will give  to the government at a special meeting  on the 28th of september after this  we'll be back in touch with you with  what the next steps are that we will be  uh considering in this process  can i just say nicolas we're coming to a  close just  thanks to all of you  who have showed an interest tonight and  for the questions that you've put  forward  we hope you find  and have found it useful to you and it's  helped inform you better to give us your  feedback can i think say thanks  particularly to jim and jared  and um to you nicola zariam so you've  done an excellent job keeping us online  and making sure that  we're keeping to time with the questions  to everyone out there take care thank  you  thanks very much dan and so that's all  we have time for tonight  if you want to know more or if you have  some feedback on what we've discussed  tonight visit the let's talk page and  let us know and that web address again  is  waimakariri.govt dot nz forward slash  let's talk  so thanks very much to our panel and  thanks so much to you at home for  joining us stay safe and have a great  evening  [Music]  you

